## 3.8 Bar chart: each state's share of cumulative procurement

**Why:** A bar chart ranks states directly. Showing each state's share of the crop's cumulative procurement, with the percentage printed on the bar, makes the concentration from Section 2.9 easy to read at a glance.

**How:** `st_share` already holds each state's cumulative total and its share within the crop. Bars are sorted with `reorder()`, and `geom_text()` prints the share on each bar.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
p8 <- ggplot(st_share, aes(x = reorder(State, share_pct), y = share_pct, fill = Crop)) +
  geom_col_interactive(aes(tooltip = tip, data_id = id), width = 0.7) +
  geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.1f%%", share_pct)), hjust = -0.15, size = 2.9) +
  coord_flip() +
  facet_wrap(~Crop, scales = "free") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = crop_cols, guide = "none") +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.2))) +
  labs(
    title = "Share of each crop's cumulative procurement, 2001-02 to 2025-26",
    subtitle = "Punjab and a few other states supply most of the cumulative total (provisional 2026-27 excluded). Rice omitted",
    caption = "Source: DFPD Central Food Grains Procurement Portal. Cumulative totals sum all reporting years, so states with longer coverage weigh more."
  ) +
  econ_theme +
  theme(
    panel.grid.major.x = element_line(colour = "#A9BFCB", linewidth = 0.4),
    panel.grid.major.y = element_blank()
  )

girafe(ggobj = p8, width_svg = 9, height_svg = 6)
```

